ut it weakens my king’s safety and creates long-term pawn structure weaknesses.  \n5. **B: c8-d7** – Develops the bishop, connects rooks, solidifies position. But it’s a passive move and misses more aggressive options.\n\nReason:  \nI want to seize the initiative. **N: f6-e4** is very tempting: it plants a knight deep in White’s territory, attacks multiple weak points (d2, f2, g3 squares), and puts immediate pressure. White has no pawn that can directly kick it (pawn on d4 is defended, pawn on f2 is behind). Also, it increases control over the center and limits White’s options. The risk is low because the knight is well-supported (pawn on d5, knight on c4, potentially queen) and not easily attacked. This move also keeps my king safe (unlike advancing g4) and avoids premature central pawn breaks that might favor White.  \n\nAnswer: N: f6-e4"}
