hite’s king (KWF1 on f2) and rook (RWF1 on c1). After king moves (likely to g1 or e1), Black captures rook next move. Material swing is significant.  \n\nReason: Material is the most critical factor here. The knight fork is forcing and wins a rook for free, improving Black’s material disadvantage to near equality. Other moves like B: f8-a3 also win a rook, but the rook on c1 is not defended and king can’t defend both attacks — but the fork on king and rook is even more forcing because it’s a direct attack on the king, forcing king move, then rook lost.  \n\nAnswer: N: e3-c2"}
