ng move — it threatens mate on the back rank (if White’s king moves, Rh1# could follow) and forces White’s king to move or defend, tying it down and possibly winning the g5 pawn later. In this simplified endgame, activating the rook to the opponent’s back rank maximizes Black’s winning chances by creating immediate threats without allowing White counterplay.\n\n**Answer:** R: h7-h1'}
