p from f2 to g3 attacks g6), but it's blocked by pawn g5? Wait, bishop f2 to g3: f2-g3 path: squares e3, f4, g5? Actually bishop moves diagonally: from f2 to g3 is diagonal f2-g3 (one square up-right) which attacks g6 only if pawn g5 is removed. Since pawn g5 is on g5, it blocks bishop's line to g6, so Bg3 is not a check, but it attacks pawn g5. So Bg3 threatens pawn and maybe later mate.  \n4. **P: b7-b5** – Prepares pawn advance, but less urgent.\n\nReason:  \nImmediate threat: win the g5 pawn to eliminate white's only promotion chance and leave white with bare king. Best is direct attack on pawn. R: h4-g4 attacks pawn, and if pawn moves or king moves, black can follow up with further attacks. Also Rg4 covers g4 square preventing white king from moving to f5/g4 easily. The rook move pressures while maintaining material advantage. Alternatively, Bg3 also attacks pawn, but rook move is stronger as it cannot be taken by king (king can't take rook because rook defended? Actually rook on g4 would be undefended, and white king could capture it on g6? No, king on g6 to g4 is not legal in one move, but pawn is on g5, so after Rg4, pawn is attacked and if pawn moves to g4, it would be captured by rook, but pawn cannot move to g4 because it's occupied by rook. So white would have to move king to defend or move pawn to g6? g6 is occupied by king. So pawn is trapped. So Rg4 wins the pawn immediately, after which white is in a hopeless position. That seems decisive.\n\nAnswer: R: h4-g4"}
