cking e6/f5 squares, and prepares to support pawn advance or threaten checks.  \n- **K: c5-d4**: King moves toward the center and closer to the pawns, potentially supporting both b3 and c4 advances.  \n- **B: d6-e5**: Attacks the knight on a8 indirectly (controls a1-h8 diagonal, but knight is not on it). However, it blocks the e5 square for own knight and king.  \n\nReason: The position is winning for Black due to two connected passed pawns (b4 and c3) vs. White's single h-pawn. The priority is to advance pawns while keeping white's king and knight at bay. The knight on c2 is passive; moving it to d4 activates it and threatens Nf5 or Ne6 in the future, which could harass the white king and help clear the path for pawns. Meanwhile, the bishop on d6 already covers the h-pawn's promotion path, so we don't need to move it urgently. King to d4 is also good, but moving the knight first improves piece coordination without risking the king unnecessarily.  \n\nAnswer: N: c2-d4"}
