far away. Even if White tries Kd7 or Ke7 to block, Black can support the pawn with Kc4 later.  \nHowever, **B: d6-c7** might be stronger first: it pins the knight to the king (since moving the knight allows Bxa8), and if White moves the knight, the pawn promotion becomes unstoppable (e.g., if Nxb6, then c2 and promote). If White plays Kd7, then Bxa8 Kxc8 still loses the knight for bishop, leaving Black up material.  \nBut moving the pawn c3-c2 first is also forcing — White must respond to the promotion threat immediately; if White moves Kd7, then c1=Q wins easily.  \nGiven the position, I think the fastest path to victory is **P: c3-c2** — it forces White into a desperate move while Black can still use king/knight/bishop to support promotion next move.  \n\n**Answer:** P: c3-c2'}
