res: g8 (but then black king can move to f7 or e7 cutting escape), g6 (but that square is attacked by black king from e6? Actually e6 does not attack g6; g6 is two squares away diagonally e6->g6 is bishop move, not king move. However, black knight could potentially cover g6 if moved to f5 or e8, not yet). Wait, g6 is not directly attacked by any black piece currently. So after B: f6-g7 check, white king could move to g6. Then black's king can move to f5 or e5 to approach, knight can try to cover. It's not an immediate checkmate but forces the king to a more active square.  \n\nAlternatively, K: e6-f7: Moves king to f7, attacking g6, g7, g8. Combined with bishop on f6, that might trap the white king in the corner. However, f7 is not check, and white king could move to g6 (but then black king on f7 attacks g6, so g6 is not safe). Actually if king moves to f7, white king is on h7, it can move to g8 (attacked by king? f7 attacks g8), or h8 (not attacked), or g6 (attacked by f7). So h8 is only safe square. Then next move black can bring bishop to g7 check, forcing king to h7 (back) then king to g8? Actually careful:  \n1. K: e6-f7 (black king to f7)  \nWhite king: moves to h8 (only safe square).  \n2. Black: B: f6-g7#? Wait, bishop to g7 is check, but white king on h8 is not attacked by bishop on g7 (g7 does not attack h8; h8 is one square diagonal away, but bishop attacks diagonals, so from g7, it attacks h8? Yes, g7 to h8 is diagonal one square, so it does attack h8). So B: f6-g7 would be checkmate if white king is on h8 and no other pieces block. Let's verify:  \nAfter 1... Kh8, black plays B: f6-g7#.  \nCheck: King on h8 is attacked by bishop on g7. Escape squares: g8 is attacked by black king on f7. So no escape. And no piece can block or capture. That's checkmate.  \n\nSo sequence: K: e6-f7 forces Kh8, then B: f6-g7#.  \n\nBut is Kh8 forced? After 1. K: e6-f7, white king could try g6?  \n1... Kf7, white king: Kg6?? But Kg6 is attacked by black king on f7 (f7 attacks g6). So not legal.  \nWhite king: Kg8?? f7 attacks g8. Not legal.  \nOnly legal move is Kh8.  \n\nSo K: e6-f7 leads to forced checkmate next move.  \n\nCompare other moves:  \nB: f6-g7 immediately gives check but allows Kg6, prolonging mate.  \nKing moves like e6-d7 or e6-f5 don't force immediate mate in one.  \nKnight moves not as direct.  \n\nReason: K: e6-f7 creates an immediate zugzwang, forcing the white king into the corner, allowing a bishop checkmate next move. It's the quickest route to checkmate.\n\nAnswer: K: e6-f7"}
