h is also attacked by white knight) and d4. Good for activity.  \n2. **K: e6-d5** – Moves king towards center and away from the white knight’s range (knight from g6 can reach e5 in one move, so moving king to d5 avoids e5).  \n3. **B: e7-f6** – Attacks the knight on g6. This seems strong: threatens the knight, and if knight moves, we gain tempo. Also defends king’s position.  \n\nThe most forcing and advantageous move is **B: e7-f6** because it directly attacks the white knight. The white knight must move or be captured, giving us initiative.  \n\nReason: Attacking the opponent's piece, especially a knight near our king, improves our position, potentially gaining material or forcing the opponent into a defensive move. In endgames, trading pieces when up material is good, but here material seems equal? Actually, we have knight, bishop, pawn vs knight? Not sure, but attacking is still good.  \n\nAnswer: B: e7-f6"}
