ps advance the pawn. White’s knight on f7 is far, so immediate danger is low. Good consolidation move.  \n\n3. **K: d5-e5** — King moves toward center/knight side but not toward pawn. This may allow White knight to harass pawn with Ne5-d3 attacking b4 if we aren’t careful. Less purposeful than c4.  \n\n4. **N: f7-d6** — Moves knight closer to center, possibly attacking b5/c4/e4 squares. Could support pawn advance indirectly, but moving the knight away from f7 reduces pressure on White’s King. In endgame with pawns, knights are better placed to block enemy king or attack pawns. Here we have no enemy pawns, so knight’s job is to restrict White’s King and threaten promotion defense.  \n\n5. **N: f7-e5** — Puts knight aggressively in center; could later go to d3 attacking b4 if needed, but right now our pawn is on b4.  \n\nConsidering we are Black and only up a pawn vs knight (practically equal material in theoretical endgame tables), the winning plan is: **use king to escort pawn to promotion while keeping White’s King and knight at bay**.  \n\nThe move **K: d5-c4** fits that plan perfectly:  \n- It walks toward the pawn.  \n- Next turn we can push b4-b5 with king on c5 supporting.  \n- If White’s knight comes to attack pawn later, king is nearby to defend.  \n- White’s King is far away on g8.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nKing centralization toward the pawn is a fundamental endgame technique. Moving pawn first (b4-b3) does not improve king position and might leave pawn vulnerable after White moves knight closer. Moving knight now doesn’t help pawn promotion as much as using king. Therefore **K: d5-c4** is the most consistent with a winning plan.\n\n**Answer:** K: d5-c4'}
