. So by the time our pawn moves b3→b2 (our 2nd move after this one), white king is at c4 or b3 to capture next move. So the pawn will be lost unless our king can shield it.  \n\nOur king is at c4, which is key: if we move our king to b5 now, we block the pawn’s path? No, pawn is on b4, king to b5 is behind pawn, not helping.  \n\nWe need king to get in front of pawn to protect its advance: for example, after b4-b3, our king could go to b4 to shield pawn from white king coming via c4.  \n\nBut our valid moves now don’t include king to b4 because c4→b4 is blocked by pawn on b4. We can move king to b5, d4, d5, etc.  \n\n**Promising moves from the list:**  \n\n1. **P: b4-b3** – pawn advances. This might let white king come closer but we still have our king near.  \n2. **K: c4-b5** – king moves backward, possibly to protect pawn from rear or reposition? Not immediate help.  \n3. **K: c4-c5** – king centralizes, maybe cuts off white king’s direct route?  \n4. **K: c4-d4** – king moves toward center, keeps options open.  \n\nBut **winning** here means promoting the pawn safely. Let’s calculate if it's possible:  \n\nIf we move **P: b4-b3**, then white’s king moves toward b2. Our king is at c4 after that. Next turn, we can move king to b4 (if pawn is on b3, king to b4 is legal because pawn not on b4 anymore). Then white king approaches: if white king is at c5, our king b4 guards pawn at b3. Pawn can then push b3-b2 (king still on b4), then white king can’t take pawn on b2 because king b4 guards it. Then b2-b1=Q.  \n\nBut this depends on white king’s speed. Let’s check distances:  \n\nFrom f7 to b2 (to attack pawn on b2) = 5 king moves. After we play P: b4-b3 now, white moves Kf7-e6 (1). Our next move: Kc4-b4. White’s 2nd move: Ke6-d5. Our 3rd move: pawn b3-b2. White’s 3rd move: Kd5-c4. Our 4th move: pawn b2-b1=Q. At this point, white king at c4, new queen on b1 checks white king. Pawn queens safely because white king can’t reach b2 before we queen.  \n\nYes – that seems to work because our king on b4 prevents white king from occupying b3 or c3 to attack b2 pawn before it queens. Let’s verify squares:  \n\nWhite’s fastest route: f7-e6-d5-c4-b3. But after our king is on b4, white king can’t go to b3 because our king controls it. So white must go c4, then b4 is occupied, so white can’t attack b2 pawn before we queen next move.  \n\nSo **P: b4-b3** is winning if followed by Kc4-b4 next turn.  \n\nIf we instead move king first (K: c4-d4 or K: c4-c5), we lose a tempo and white king gets closer, possibly making the pawn race lost.  \n\n**Best move: P: b4-b3**, forcing pawn advance while our king is perfectly placed to shield next move.\n\n**Reason:**  \nThe pawn advance is urgent and winning with correct follow-up because our king can protect the pawn’s queening square in time. Any king move now wastes a crucial tempo, allowing white king to get closer and prevent promotion.  \n\n**Answer:** P: b4-b3"}
