olid because it places the king directly in front of the pawn, controlling b2 and protecting the pawn.\n\n3. **K: c3-b4** – King moves to b4, which also defends the pawn on b3 and prepares to move toward b3 or a5, possibly cutting off White’s king.\n\n4. **K: c3-c2** – King moves backward, away from the pawn. This seems passive and not helpful for promotion.\n\n5. **K: c3-c4** – King moves to c4, centralizing and defending b3 from a distance. Also controls d5 and e4 squares.\n\n6. **K: c3-d2** – King moves away from the pawn and central area, less active.\n\n7. **K: c3-d3** – King stays on the same rank but moves to d3, still defends c4 and e4, but not directly supporting pawn.\n\n8. **K: c3-d4** – King moves to d4, more central and can block White’s king approach.\n\nConsidering that the pawn is key, moving it immediately might be premature without king support because White’s king can head toward b2. For example, after P: b3-b2, White king could go to c5, then b4, threatening to capture the pawn. My king at c3 could move to b4 to defend, but that’s only possible if I move the king first.\n\nBetter plan: First move the king to support the pawn, then advance the pawn. The most direct is **K: c3-b2**, placing the king in front of the pawn. This defends b3 and now the pawn can safely move to b2 next turn, with the king already on b2 (so pawn would move to b1 and promote). However, note that when the pawn is on b2 and king on b2, that’s not possible because two pieces can’t occupy the same square. So actually, after K: c3-b2, the pawn on b3 cannot move to b2 because the king is there. That means the pawn would be stuck until the king moves away. That’s a problem.\n\nCheck: If I play K: c3-b2, then the pawn is stuck because b2 is occupied. So to advance the pawn, I’d need to move the king away first, wasting time. So that’s not ideal.\n\nAlternative: **K: c3-b4** – Then the pawn can move to b2 (since b2 is empty), and then my king can move to b3 or a3 to support the pawn’s final push. That seems more flexible. After K: c3-b4, if White’s king moves toward us, I can bring my king to c3 or a3 to defend.\n\nAlso, **K: c3-c4** is similar but less directly shielding the pawn from the white king coming from the d-file.\n\nLooking at the geometry: White king at d6. To capture the pawn on b3, it would need to go to c5, b4, or a4, etc. If I play K: c3-b4, I directly block b4, making it harder for White’s king to approach the pawn. Also, my king on b4 defends a5, c5, and b3. So that seems good.\n\nAnother idea: Perhaps advancing the pawn immediately is okay because after P: b3-b2, White’s king is too far to catch it? Let’s count: From d6 to b2: d6-c5-b4-a3-b2 (or d6-c5-b4-b3-b2) is 4 moves (d6-c5, c5-b4, b4-a3, a3-b2). My pawn only needs 1 move to b2, then 1 move to b1 to promote. So in 2 moves I can promote. White king needs at least 4 moves to reach b2. So if I move the pawn immediately, I can promote before White’s king can capture it. However, after promotion, White’s king might try to capture my new queen, but with my king nearby, it should be safe.\n\nBut note: After P: b3-b2, White king could go to c5, then b4, threatening to capture the pawn on b2 if my king is not defending. My king on c3 could move to c4 or b3 to defend. Actually, if I play P: b3-b2, then my king can move to c4 or b3 next turn to protect b2. For example, after P: b3-b2, White king moves to c5; then I play K: c3-b3, defending b2. Then White king moves to b5; I play P: b2-b1=Q. So it seems promoting is straightforward.\n\nHowever, if I move the king first to b4, I might even gain a tempo by threatening to advance the pawn with king protection. But moving the king to b4 might allow White’s king to come closer via c6 or d5.\n\nGiven the distance, the simplest and fastest is to advance the pawn immediately. After promotion, I’ll have a queen vs king, which is a winning endgame.\n\nSo I think the best move is **P: b3-b2**.\n\nReason: The pawn is two steps away from promotion, and White’s king is four steps away from capturing it. By moving the pawn immediately, I start the promotion race and will likely queen before White can prevent it. My king can then move to support the new queen and assist in checkmating.\n\nAnswer: P: b3-b2'}
